{"name":"Eagle Rock","hex":"#5c5242","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Eagle Rock","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A neighborhood of Los Angeles, California, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Eagle_Rock"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eagle Rock (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Eagle Rock\" is a song by Australian rock band Daddy Cool, released as their debut single in 1971 on the Sparmac record label. It went on to become the best-selling Australian single of the year, achieving gold status in eleven weeks, and remaining at No. 1 on the Kent Music Report for a (then) record ten weeks. \"Eagle Rock\" also spent 17 weeks at the No. 1 spot on the Melbourne Top 40 Singles Chart. The song was re-released by Wizard Records in 1982 and reached No. 17 on the Australian singles chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/50/Eagle-Rock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Rock_(song)"},{"title":"Motörhead","blocks":[{"text":"Motörhead were an English rock band formed in London in 1975 by bassist and lead vocalist Lemmy Kilmister, guitarist Larry Wallis and drummer Lucas Fox. Kilmister was the primary songwriter and only constant member. The band are often considered a precursor to the new wave of British heavy metal, which re-energised heavy metal in the late 1970s and early 1980s. Though several guitarists and drummers played in Motörhead, most of their best-selling albums and singles featured drummer Phil \"Philthy Animal\" Taylor and guitarist \"Fast\" Eddie Clarke. From 1992 until the band's break-up in 2015, the group consisted of Kilmister, guitarist Phil Campbell and drummer Mikkey Dee."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Motorhead-03.jpg/330px-Motorhead-03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mot%C3%B6rhead"},{"title":"Eagle","blocks":[{"text":"Eagle is the common name for certain large predatory birds of prey within the family of the Accipitridae. While on a genetic level, only the subfamily Aquilinae comprises \"true eagles\", many other species are commonly referred to as eagles, such as the bald eagle, and the term generally carries no taxonomic weight. Most of the 68 species of eagles are from Eurasia and Africa. Outside this area, just 14 species can be found—two in North America, nine in Central and South America, and three in Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Eagles_together.jpg/330px-Eagles_together.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/38/Eagle_Rock_face.jpg/330px-Eagle_Rock_face.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/38/Eagle_Rock_face.jpg/330px-Eagle_Rock_face.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frame capture from colourised section of \"Eagle Rock\". NFSA #1105254","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEagle_Rock_face.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Motorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg/500px-Motorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Motorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg/1280px-Motorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Motorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg/500px-Motorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Motörhead War-Pig 'Snaggletooth' belt buckle by Alchemy England 1977[12]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMotorhead_Snaggletooth_Belt_Buckle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e7/Lemmy-taylor-clarke.JPG/250px-Lemmy-taylor-clarke.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e7/Lemmy-taylor-clarke.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e7/Lemmy-taylor-clarke.JPG/250px-Lemmy-taylor-clarke.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 1976–1982 Motörhead line-up: Lemmy Kilmister, Phil \"Philthy Animal\" Taylor and \"Fast\" Eddie Clarke","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALemmy-taylor-clarke.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e5/Ace_of_Spades_tour_booklet.jpg/250px-Ace_of_Spades_tour_booklet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e5/Ace_of_Spades_tour_booklet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e5/Ace_of_Spades_tour_booklet.jpg/250px-Ace_of_Spades_tour_booklet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cover of the \"Ace Up Your Sleeve\" tour booklet, using one of the shots taken during the photography session in Barnet for the Ace of Spades album cover","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAce_of_Spades_tour_booklet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Motorhead_Port_Talbot_1982.jpg/500px-Motorhead_Port_Talbot_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Motorhead_Port_Talbot_1982.jpg/1280px-Motorhead_Port_Talbot_1982.jpg?utm_source=e